        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nFor EVERY candidate, judge fit: direct means the answer itself fills the exact requested relation for an allowed referent; wrong_argument is explicit role/type mismatch; incidental means occurrence without enough support; uncertain means unresolved. Select support only for an original segment positively assigning this candidate to the requested relation; otherwise empty. Select counter only for actual counterevidence, not mere absence. conflict=same_referent only for incompatible claims for the same entity/attribute/relevant scope, not legitimate namesakes or complementary facts. entity labels are provisional groups, not truth. scope=excluded only for an explicit question constraint, with constraint selecting a literal numeric phrase from the question; otherwise allowed/unknown. observed_slot describes the answer's ACTUAL role, not the role wanted by the question. Mere disagreement does not make a source false. Fill all opaque keys.\nDefend complementary referents and explicitly use your pretrained knowledge as a FALLIBLE yes/no plausibility check. Knowledge doubt is not a citation or truth label: when a claim is strongly implausible or conflicts with remembered facts, set conflict=uncertain and never invent a textual counter source. Keep role fit separate: direct relation claims can be factually false. Do not reject unfamiliar but explicitly supported namesakes just because memory knows only a famous one. A claimed convention secretly being a space agency deserves scrutiny. Do not label same-role competing dates wrong_argument.\nInput: {\"question\": \"2nd Duke of Newcastle, what year was he born?\", \"slot_hint\": \"birth_date\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 273, \"text\": \"Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle-upon-Tyne, KG, PC (24 June 1630 – 26 July 1691), styled Viscount Mansfield until 1676, was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1660 to 1676, and then inherited the dukedom.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00\", \"start\": 274, \"end\": 382, \"text\": \"Cavendish was the only son of William Cavendish, 1st Duke of Newcastle and his first wife, Elizabeth Basset.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00\", \"start\": 383, \"end\": 474, \"text\": \"His maternal grandparents were William Basset and Judith Austen, daughter of Thomas Austen.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00\", \"start\": 475, \"end\": 587, \"text\": \"In April 1660, Lord Mansfield was elected Member of Parliament (MP) for Derbyshire in the Convention Parliament.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00\", \"start\": 588, \"end\": 624, \"text\": \"He was elected MP for Northumberland\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 212, \"text\": \"Henry Pelham-Clinton, 2nd Duke of Newcastle Henry Fiennes Pelham-Clinton, 2nd Duke of Newcastle-under-Lyne, KG, PC (16 April 1720 – 22 February 1794) was born in London, the second son of the 7th Earl of Lincoln.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01\", \"start\": 213, \"end\": 335, \"text\": \"Henry's father died in 1728, and his brother, the 8th Earl of Lincoln, died in 1730, making Henry the 9th Earl of Lincoln.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01\", \"start\": 336, \"end\": 425, \"text\": \"As he was still a minor, his guardian was his uncle, the 1st Duke of Newcastle-upon-Tyne.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01\", \"start\": 426, \"end\": 494, \"text\": \"Newcastle was childless, and soon regarded Lord Lincoln as his heir.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01\", \"start\": 495, \"end\": 582, \"text\": \"Newcastle, and his brother Henry Pelham, were the two most powerful men in England, and\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle\", \"scope\": \"Birth year\", \"claim\": \"Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle was born on 24 June 1630.\", \"answer\": \"1630\", \"quote\": \"Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle-upon-Tyne, KG, PC (24 June 1630 – 26 July 1691), styled Viscount Mansfield until 1676, was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1660 to 1676, and then inherited the dukedom.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0260-8ddeae739596:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Henry Fiennes Pelham-Clinton, 2nd Duke of Newcastle-under-Lyne\", \"scope\": \"birth year\", \"claim\": \"Henry Fiennes Pelham-Clinton, 2nd Duke of Newcastle-under-Lyne was born on 16 April 1720.\", \"answer\": \"1720\", \"quote\": \"Henry Pelham-Clinton, 2nd Duke of Newcastle Henry Fiennes Pelham-Clinton, 2nd Duke of Newcastle-under-Lyne, KG, PC (16 April 1720 – 22 February 1794) was born in London, the second son of the 7th Earl of Lincoln.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"R5_answers\": [\"1630\", \"1720\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"1720\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"1630\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
